version: 5
title: Keep highlighted code in view when panels resize
opening:
  title: Resizing a panel should not lose the highlighted code
  summary: >-
    A tour reveals a line of code. You resize a panel, and that line can move out of view—even
    though you never scrolled. This fix changes which editor events trigger linked scrolling. Follow
    the unwanted path, the guard that blocks it, and the tests that keep normal scrolling intact.
conclusion:
  title: Keep redraws; synchronize only actual movement
  summary: >-
    The handler now distinguishes a changed scrollable area from a changed scroll position.
    Layout-only events redraw the connectors and stop; unsuppressed movement still synchronizes. The
    tests establish that routing distinction. They do not reproduce a resize, so the final visual
    check is whether highlighted code stays in view when a panel changes size.
range:
  base: a50c2a6f58a2dfd7f85039105a27e4b92b98a3f7
  head: 901a193203e4840dbf6e06c6a273fac7f2d713bd
anchors:
  before-guard:
    file: media/script.js
    revision: base
    contains: |2-
          editor.onDidScrollChange(() => {
              if (suppressEditorEvents) {
                  connectorController.scheduleDrawConnections();
                  return;
              }

              if (editorMode === MODE_MULTI_WAY) {
                  synchronizeMultiScroll(editor);
              } else {
                  synchronizeEditorScroll(editor);
              }
              connectorController.scheduleDrawConnections();
          });
  ignore-layout:
    file: media/script.js
    revision: head
    contains: |2-
              if (suppressEditorEvents || (!event.scrollTopChanged && !event.scrollLeftChanged)) {
                  connectorController.scheduleDrawConnections();
                  return;
              }
  width-test:
    file: test/runTests.js
    revision: head
    contains: |2-
          assert.deepEqual(
              invokeScrollHandler({ scrollTopChanged: false, scrollLeftChanged: false, scrollWidthChanged: true }),
              ['draw']
          );
  real-scroll-tests:
    file: test/runTests.js
    revision: head
    contains: |2-
          assert.deepEqual(
              invokeScrollHandler({ scrollTopChanged: true, scrollLeftChanged: false }),
              ['two-way-scroll', 'draw']
          );
          assert.deepEqual(
              invokeScrollHandler({ scrollTopChanged: false, scrollLeftChanged: true }, 'multi-way'),
              ['multi-scroll', 'draw']
          );
connections: []
chapters:
  - id: focus
    title: Preserve code focus
    scenes:
      - id: marker-focus
        title: Why a resize can move code—and where to stop it
        summary: >-
          The scroll callback also reports changes in how much content can be scrolled. The old
          handler does not inspect that distinction. We’ll compare it with the new guard, then test
          the layout-only case and real movement separately.
        takeaway: >-
          Filter the event before synchronization; preserve the redraw and the normal scrolling
          path.
        steps:
          - id: before-guard
            title: The old handler treats an unsuppressed event as movement
            body: >-
              After the suppression check, every event reaches a synchronizer selected by panel
              mode. The handler never asks whether the position moved. The fix’s source comment
              identifies the consequence: synchronizing a layout-only notification can push a
              revealed code anchor out of view.
            focus: before-guard
          - id: ignore-layout
            title: Redraw layout changes without moving the other panels
            body: >-
              The new condition asks whether either scroll position changed. If neither did, it
              redraws the lines connecting matching code and returns before synchronization.
              Existing event suppression also takes this early exit. Unsuppressed movement still
              reaches the synchronizer, so fixing the resize case does not require disabling linked
              scrolling.
            focus: ignore-layout
          - id: width-test
            title: A wider scrollable area must not move the code
            body: >-
              The test changes the scrollable width while leaving both position flags false. It
              expects only a connector redraw. Calling a synchronizer here would reopen the route by
              which layout changes can disturb a code reveal.
            focus: width-test
          - id: real-scroll-tests
            title: Real movement must still keep panels together
            body: >-
              Skipping synchronization for every scroll event would pass the previous check but break linked scrolling.
              These assertions rule that out: vertical movement in two-panel mode and horizontal
              movement in multi-panel mode each synchronize, then redraw. Panel mode selects the
              synchronizer; the direction of movement does not. These tests check callback routing,
              not on-screen marker visibility.
            focus: real-scroll-tests
        bullets: []
        tags:
          - scrolling
          - bug-fix
